1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,959
Writing is the second step in this three step sequence and this is a fantastic way to help you organize your thoughts better.
写作是这个三步流程的第二步；她认为，这是帮助你更好地组织想法的好方法。

2
00:00:08,959 --> 00:00:16,000
Get it on paper so you can actually see whether the thoughts you have in your mind are truly organized or not.
把想法写到纸上，这样你就能真正看清，脑海里的想法到底有没有条理。

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,799
It also helps you to identify your thoughts.
她认为，这也能帮助你辨认自己的想法。

4
00:00:18,799 --> 00:00:27,270
So sometimes when you have those thoughts in your mind they're all muddled it's hard to identify clearly what those thoughts are.
有时脑海里那些想法混成一团，很难清楚辨别它们究竟是什么。

5
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:34,079
Writing is one of the best ways you can get clear on those thoughts and identify them too.
她认为，写作是理清并辨认那些想法的最佳方式之一。

6
00:00:34,079 --> 00:00:38,480
As I mentioned at the beginning of this video our speech is a reflection of our thinking.
正如我在视频开头提到的，我们说的话反映了我们的思考。

7
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:45,519
So we first need to get our thoughts organized and we do this by writing before we actually jump into speaking.
所以，我们首先得把想法组织好，而在真正开口说之前，可以通过写作来做到。

8
00:00:45,519 --> 00:00:48,560
Which is the next step we'll talk about in a couple of moments.
口头表达是下一步，我们再过一会儿就会谈到。

9
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:52,959
When you don't do this this is what leads to muddled communication.
她认为，如果没有先这么做，就会导致混乱的沟通。

10
00:00:52,959 --> 00:00:58,560
When you have those muddled thoughts in your mind and then you go from those muddled thoughts straight to speaking.
当脑海里的想法还很混乱，你就直接从这些混乱的想法跳到开口表达。

11
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:06,880
That's what leads to muddled speaking and not being able to articulate your thoughts clearly or communicate them well.
她认为，这就会让话说得混乱，无法清晰表达想法，也无法好好传达。

12
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:14,229
Basically what writing does is it allows you to take the time to organize your thoughts well.
基本上，写作的作用就是让你花时间，把想法好好组织起来。

13
00:01:14,239 --> 00:01:22,880
It allows you to draw them from your mind to put them on paper and to see whether they are organized or not and whether they're good thoughts or not.
它让你把想法从脑海里提取出来，写到纸上，看看它们是否有条理，以及是不是好的想法。

14
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:32,400
You can put those thoughts in order you can organize them and you can eliminate thoughts that aren't important or that don't add anything to the discussion.
你可以把想法排好顺序、组织起来，再删掉不重要或对讨论没有帮助的想法。

15
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:41,200
If you feel that you don't make sense when you speak well it's probably because you haven't spent the time organizing your thoughts like you do through this writing process.
如果你觉得说话时别人听不懂，她认为，可能是因为你没像写作时那样花时间整理想法。

16
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:49,120
You've just jumped straight into speaking without organizing them first and that is what has led to muddled communication and you not making sense.
你没有先整理，就直接开口；她认为，这会导致沟通混乱，让人不明白你的意思。

17
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:53,520
I want you to get into the habit of writing your thoughts now how do you do this.
我希望你养成把想法写下来的习惯；那么具体怎么做呢？

18
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:59,599
Well it should be something that you do every day just like reading part of your everyday routine.
这应该像阅读一样，是你每天做的事情，成为日常作息的一部分。

19
00:01:59,599 --> 00:02:03,750
And it's something that can directly follow your reading schedule.
而且，它可以直接安排在阅读之后。

20
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:10,320
You can do journal writing you can do formal writing whatever type of writing suits you better.
你可以写日记，也可以正式写作，选择更适合自己的类型。

21
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:13,200
I recommend you write one page every day.
我建议你每天写一页。

22
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:20,159
So reading is five to ten pages every day but writing because it takes more time you are allowed to pause.
阅读是每天五到十页；不过写作更花时间，你可以停下来思考。

23
00:02:20,159 --> 00:02:25,279
You're allowed to organize your thoughts a lot more write one page per day.
你可以更充分地整理自己的想法；每天写一页就好。

24
00:02:25,279 --> 00:02:33,680
I usually do my writing in a book much like this it is just a plain notebook I'll open it up.
我通常就在一本像这样的本子上写；它只是普通的笔记本，我把它打开给你看。

25
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:39,200
The pages are playing there is no lines whatsoever in this book.
这里字幕说“pages are playing”，疑似想说页面空白；她接着说明，这本子完全没有横线。

26
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:45,520
And I find that this works much better for me to not have lines drawn on the page.
我发现，页面上不画横线，对我来说要好用得多。

27
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:51,520
I find that when I am working or writing with lines it limits my thinking in a way.
我发现，工作或写作时如果页面有横线，在某种程度上会限制我的思考。

28
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:55,839
It prevents me from thinking freely from writing freely.
它让我无法自由思考、自由书写。

29
00:02:55,839 --> 00:03:06,230
So I like to have the the space and the opportunity on a blank page like this to just scribble down my thoughts and write them in a more coherent way.
所以，我喜欢这样的空白页提供的空间与机会，可以随手记下想法，再把它们写得更连贯。
